= Description logic — Technical =

== Interpretation ==
A DL interpretation:

<pre>
I = (Δ^I, ·^I)
</pre>

contains nonempty domain Δ and interpretation function.

Concept name A maps to:

<pre>
A^I ⊆ Δ^I
</pre>

Role name r maps to:

<pre>
r^I ⊆ Δ^I × Δ^I
</pre>

Individual a maps to:

<pre>
a^I ∈ Δ^I
</pre>

== ALC constructors ==
For concepts C,D:

<pre>
(C ⊓ D)^I = C^I ∩ D^I
(C ⊔ D)^I = C^I ∪ D^I
(¬C)^I = Δ^I \ C^I
(∃r.C)^I = {x | ∃y ((x,y)∈r^I ∧ y∈C^I)}
(∀r.C)^I = {x | ∀y ((x,y)∈r^I → y∈C^I)}
</pre>

ALC forms a foundational expressive DL.

== Reasoning problems ==
Core tasks:
* concept satisfiability;
* subsumption;
* knowledge-base consistency;
* instance checking;
* realization;
* classification.

Many tasks reduce to satisfiability.

== Complexity ==
Reasoning complexity depends on the DL.

Expressive features such as:
* number restrictions;
* inverse roles;
* nominals;
* role chains;
* transitivity;

can increase complexity and constrain allowed role combinations.

== Tableau reasoning ==
Many DL reasoners use tableau-style algorithms.

They expand constraints while checking whether a clash-free model can be constructed.

Optimizations include:
* blocking;
* caching;
* dependency-directed backtracking;
* absorption.

